From a301a424e5f6e9aa4ca36f4104f4cde72cdea6d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:50:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] fix(plugins): declare in-process isolation honestly

Every shipped manifest declared `isolation: restricted-rpc`, but the
host has no process boundary: plugins are imported into the control
plane process and IsolationGuard is only a policy table that plugin
code is never forced through. decision-typesafe-jev made the mismatch
concrete by calling the TypeSafe API with urllib while claiming a mode
that forbids direct network access.

Until a real boundary exists (subprocess plus RPC, or containers),
PluginSpec and the plugin-manifest schema accept only `in-process`
(unset still means in-process) and reject restricted-rpc and sandbox
with an explanation. All ten manifests, the single-node example and
the scaffold template now declare in-process; typesafe-jev keeps
requesting network.egress, which matches what it does. The SDK
warning for an unset isolation no longer promises a restricted-rpc
default, and the v1.1 conformance test checks in-process plugins by
loading their declared entrypoint.

This does not add any isolation; it only stops manifests from claiming
one.

Breaking: manifests declaring restricted-rpc or sandbox are rejected.
